    "### 11. Tensor Contraction and Einstein Notation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "A = np.random.randn(2, 3)\n",
    "B = np.random.randn(3, 4)\n",
    "C_matmul = np.einsum('ij,jk->ik', A, B)\n",
    "\n",
    "A_batch = np.random.randn(5, 2, 3)\n",
    "B_batch = np.random.randn(5, 3, 4)\n",
    "C_batch = np.einsum('bij,bjk->bik', A_batch, B_batch)\n",
    "\n",
    "T = np.random.randn(2, 2, 2)\n",
    "S = np.random.randn(2, 2, 2)\n",
    "Contracted = np.einsum('ijk,klm->ijlm', T, S)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Matmul shape:\", C_matmul.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Batch matmul shape:\", C_batch.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Contraction shape:\", Contracted.shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 12. Kronecker Product in Deep Learning\n",
    "The Kronecker product $A \\otimes B$ of matrices $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ and $B \\in \\mathbb{R}^{p \\times q}$ is a block matrix of size $mp \\times nq$. \n",
    "In K-FAC, the Fisher Information Matrix of a linear layer $Y = W X$ is approximated by the Kronecker product of the covariance of inputs and the covariance of upstream gradients: $F \\approx S \\otimes A$. This avoids computing and inverting the massive exact FIM, because the inverse of a Kronecker product is the Kronecker product of the inverses: $(S \\otimes A)^{-1} = S^{-1} \\otimes A^{-1}$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Theoretical explanation."
   ]
